after many years of rumors and waiting  metroid 5 aka metroid dread is finally  releasing on the nintendo switch metroid  is a series that's been around since the  famicom and nes days but never quite  reached the same mainstream recognition  as mario or zelda but there's been  somewhat of a metroid resurgence lately  samus returns on 3ds prime 4 in  development and now metroid dread which  wraps up the main story started all the  way back in the original game even the  announcement trailer for metroid dread  has over 2 million views on nintendo's  youtube channel as of making this video  but if you're looking to finally give  metroid a shot you might be asking  yourself well does the original 8-bit  game hold up is it worth going all the  way back to the start before dread to  see how it all began well in this video  i'm going to answer that question as i  revisit the original metroid the  challenge is metroid the power is  nintendo defend the planet zebus against  the evil mother brain it's survival or  destruction to battle or die metroid  only from nintendo for those who don't  know the original metroid has a very  simple premise defeat the metroid and  destroy the mother brain but if you flip  through the pages of the original  instruction booklet you get a little  more backstory to boil it down as best i  can galactic federation exists and  there's peace and prosperity between the  planets but space pirates exist and they  don't want peace so the galactic  federation police has formed to stop  them but the space pirates were too cool  and too awesome to be caught so space  hunters or bounty hunters were sent to  capture them in the year 20x5 the space  pirates steal a capsule from a research  lab containing an unknown deadly life  form that was dubbed metroid the space  pirates who hate peace and happiness  want to use the metroid as a weapon to  destroy the galaxy how original the  federation police then locate the space  pirates headquarters on a planet known  as zebus or well zebeth according to the  original game's translation the  federation police are unable to get into  the planet as a last resort the  federation police call in the best space  hunter in the galaxy samus aaron the  game's protagonist to penetrate the  center of the planet and destroy the  leader of the space pirates mother brain  so uh  guess the story isn't so simple after  all  if you're curious about the entire  metroid timeline in the lead-up to  metroid dread well then you're in luck  because we have the complete metroid  timeline explained from our very own  adam mason which i will link in the  description metroid was created after  nintendo's other big hits like donkey  kong super mario bros and the legend of  zelda in an effort to distinguish  metroid from other titles it was decided  that the game would be a non-linear  platforming adventure game with a design  that both encouraged exploration and  collecting new permanent upgrades to  expand your arsenal the title metroid  comes from the combination of the words  metro and android metro because the  planet's design takes inspiration from  the japanese metro subway lines and  android because according to the  instruction manual mentioned earlier  samus is a cyborg their entire body has  been surgically strengthened with  robotics giving them superpowers  notice how i changed the pronouns in  that quote well it's no secret nowadays  but back in the day if you finished  metroid fast enough it would be revealed  to the player that underneath the suit  samus was actually a woman whether the  original instruction booklet called sam  as a hee because the person writing it  didn't know or they didn't want to spoil  the surprise we'll never really know as  the story goes this happened because the  production of metroid was a very free  working environment someone just said  hey wouldn't it be kind of cool if it  turned out the person inside the suit  was a woman sure the original 1979 alien  film was a big influence on metroid and  the protagonist of that film is also a  woman but the protagonist of metroid a  brand new video game from the biggest  video game company of the time being one  of the first female protagonists in  video games was a big deal especially in  1986 and samus is considered to be a  breakthrough for female characters in  video games to this day so not only did  metroid go on to inspire the  metroidvania genre as seen in video  games like hollow knight and axiom verge  it was also among the first games to  feature a female protagonist so despite  how anyone feels about actually playing  this game it's definitely an important  one in the history of video games and  all that's to say  i didn't really like playing the  original metroid or rather i don't feel  like the game is aged very well it's not  just because the game is old there are  plenty of nes games that i think are  still fun like the original super mario  bros but with metroid what was once  innovative now feels like a relic of the  past at the end of the day it's not  right default in nes game for what it  isn't so i'm not going to do that rather  here's why i don't recommend forcing  yourself through the original metroid in  2021 for starters this game is tough  feel free to tell me in the comments  that i'm not a real gamer but with samus  only being able to aim up left or right  it can be hard to hit anything that  isn't exactly lined up with you and you  can only move so fast meaning dodging  isn't always the best solution either if  you die you go all the way back to the  beginning or at least all the way back  to the beginning of a new zone since the  game has multiple some people might  enjoy the challenge but i really don't  like being forced to traverse back to  the same place over and over every time  i die and start back from square one you  also start the game with only 30 health  and 30 might seem like a big number but  it's really not a lot when the max is 99  it can be upgraded by finding energy  tanks and you keep those energy tanks  even when you die when you do die it  still goes back down to 30 and those  extra energy tanks are just empty want  more health better get used to farming  enemies for five extra health at a time  given the fact that drops are random  this could take a while or you could  just try to power your way through with  dirty health it's kind of a lose-lose  scenario the game also has no map and a  lot of areas look exactly the same so  it's really easy to get lost it's one  thing to get booted back to the  beginning it's another to have no clear  indication of how to even get back to  where you were plus upgrades and secrets  are so easy to miss and rarely is there  ever any indication that there's a  secret to be found bomb this floor how  would you know to do that this red door  only opens when you shoot it with five  missiles again how would you know to do  that trial and error i don't consider  shooting and bombing every single corner  trial and error just a waste of time but  maybe that's just me oh and those  missiles i mentioned there's a finite  amount want more time to farm enemies  for random drops just like health and  that could take a really long time so if  anything i'm less incentivized to blow  up everything around me when my missiles  are precious resources my capture card  didn't even like this game it kept  smearing for some reason in my opinion  one of the only redeeming qualities of  this game is the music it's so iconic  and never gets tiring even if i was  stuck for a while or kept dying and  trying to get back to where i was and  trust me there was a lot of that  [Music]  at this point you might be thinking  yourself well now why is evan so  negative it's a video game from 35 years  ago what did he expect well first of all  you're not entirely wrong but there's a  very specific reason i lean a little  more on the negative side with metroid  and it consists of two additional words  wanna guess what they are  [Music]  in 2004 nintendo released a full remake  of the original metroid titled zero  mission for the game boy advance metroid  zero mission is a full retelling of the  original story with new cutscenes and an  entirely new ending section starring  zero suit samus so if you're looking to  start with the first entry before  metroid dread this is actually the  perfect place to start but it's not just  an improved story oh no this game has  all the bells and whistles save points  so you don't have to go all the way back  to the beginning when you die check a  map  check a system that gives you a  direction to work towards check  difficulty options check being able to  aim your arm cannon in more than three  stiff directions check and here's the  best part once you beat the game you  unlock the original nes metroid yeah  it's included in zero mission two games  in one so if you want to start from zero  mission and play that you also get to  try the original nes metroid for free  the original metroid was a perfect  candidate for a remake and i'm glad  nintendo acted upon that 17 years ago so  okay does the original metroid hold up  in 2021  no  absolutely not play zero mission instead  unfortunately despite just singing the  praises of metroid zero mission there's  only two legal ways to play the game  nowadays you can either track down an  original gba cartridge or you can  download it from the wii u virtual  console i always knew owning a wii u  would be cool one day currently on the  nintendo switch you can only play the  original nes metroid through their  online service but it's very strange to  me that they went out of their way to  make a really solid remake all those  years ago but now only offer the  original inferior version but i will  give credit where credit is due there  are two special versions of metroid on  the switches nes online that start you  later in the game and with a bunch of  upgrades already unlocked so if you  wanted to just dip your toes into the  original metroid this isn't a bad way to  do it since there's a zero mission  shaped hole in the switches library so  in conclusion i really wouldn't call the  original metroid a bad game far from it  actually sure it's not worth playing  nowadays especially with how much better  each subsequent entry in the series is  but the impact the original metroid made  was tremendous and will forever be an  important milestone in video games if  you're looking forward to metroid dread  then consider subscribing to our channel  as we'll have a video review and plenty  of other stuff surrounding the game's  release we've even got a few metroid  videos up now like the metroid timeline  mentioned earlier and a metroid buyer's  guide by john luke if you're wondering  which metroid games to play outside of  the two i spoke about in this video and  if you liked this video i did another  one in the same format titled revisiting  the original no more heroes back in  august which i'll link in the  description below and if there are any  other series you'd like to see covered  in the revisiting the original style  definitely let me know in the comments  as well and hey if you made it this far  and you're listening to me right now  thank you so much for watching i really  do appreciate it for all things gaming  keep it here on gamespot  [Music]  you

